    "narrative": "The offensive outlook for Ghost Ride the WHIP centers on three high-ceiling plays today: **James Wood** (76.28), **Mickey Moniak** (75.98), and **Corbin Carroll** (74.38). **James Wood** earns top billing despite a 45% rain risk, as his elite park factors (+16%) and the high likelihood of swiping bags against a slow-delivering pitcher make him the primary engine for our 5x5 production. Similarly, **Mickey Moniak** is positioned for a breakout game batting second against Taj Bradley; he benefits from a massive 14% boost due to dynamic platoon splits and a perfect environment for stolen base attempts. **Corbin Carroll** remains a perennial start, leveraging a favorable matchup against Nick Martinez, where an elite SP skill advantage (+16%) offsets the negative park factors. These three provide the necessary speed-to-power equilibrium required to maintain our competitive edge in stolen bases.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench today involves **Seiya Suzuki**, who landed at a 45.43 efficiency score. While his pedigree suggests a higher ceiling, he was squeezed out by a brutal matchup against Jacob Misiorowski, whose stifling SP skill rating (-19%) severely dampened **Seiya Suzuki**\u2019s base projection. Ultimately, the optimizer favored **Tyler Soderstrom** (67.58) and **Jose Caballero** (65.50) due to their platoon advantages and superior situational matchups against pitchers like Walbert Ure\u00f1a and Payton Tolle. Owners should monitor the rain risk for **James Wood** and **Jose Caballero** closely; should either game be postponed, **Seiya Suzuki** would be the immediate beneficiary to plug back into the active rotation, though the risk of losing the daily cap on a low-efficiency floor is currently too high.",
